**Bus Voltage Ripple & Filtering:** "But before I finish talking about it, I want to comment on something that needs to be discussed here. This bus voltage, besides being fed back negatively here... This inverter is a single-phase inverter; it has a characteristic of a full-wave rectifier. It's like this: this inverter, as it's a single-phase inverter, if we simplify it here as a single-phase full-wave rectifier circuit, you have the rectifier bridge, the capacitor, and here a resistor representing the load. The voltage on the rectified bus will have a DC value plus a ripple. This ripple will have a frequency of twice the grid frequency. In our case, 120 Hz. So I will see a 120 Hz ripple here. If I increase the load (decrease the resistor), my ripple increases. If I increase my capacitor, my ripple decreases. But you reach a point where you can't just keep increasing your capacitance. So what will I do so that this ripple doesn't disturb my control? Because it is a disturbance in the bus voltage, this 120 Hz ripple. What I will do is, before performing this negative feedback, I will pass this measured voltage through a 120 Hz notch filter. So how will this look? It will look like this: I will take the bus voltage, pass it through the 120 Hz synchronized notch filter. So all that ripple at 120 Hz will be cut, and here I will only see the DC value. So if my bus voltage should be at 28 volts, it will have this shape here. It will have this 120 Hz dip, and since I have my notch filter, I will only see this DC component. Therefore, I won't have a disturbance in my control."

**MPPT Algorithm:** "Now, for the MPPT in the Boost, I will have an MPPT algorithm. This is the 'Perturb and Observe' method, in its simplest form. There are various algorithms, but what it basically does is: it multiplies the PV panel voltage (Vpv) times the PV current (Ipv) – this is your photovoltaic power. It keeps comparing this power, whether it is increasing or decreasing. And each time this calculation is made, it will request an increase in my Boost reference current. This will give an increment. If the power is increasing with each current increase, then I'm following the right path. It's a very simple algorithm that I implemented here. And then I pass it through a PI controller to control this current, and the PI output is the PWM duty cycle for the Boost. The rest of the circuit remains in this form."

**MPPT Operation Description:** "So here you have the increments; you keep increasing your current. You start at this point, you give a reference current, it rises, your power increases until it reaches this point. Then if you ask to increase your current further with small increments, but you see that your power starts to decrease, it means you have passed the Maximum Power Point. So now you start to decrease your reference current. And it goes back. It will always be here, hovering around that peak. So this is a brief description of the MPPT algorithm."

